","how":"Breakfast cereal and bread crust. Corn whiskey and young craft whiskeys show it most."}},{"key":"smoke","label":"Smoke","scale":["None","L","M","H"],"tags":"smoke","spotlight":["scotch"],"spotlightNote":"Peat smoke is common in Scotch, especially from Islay.","tip":{"what":"Smoke aromas: usually peat, from malted barley dried over peat fires. Can read as campfire, ash, iodine, seaweed or medicinal.","how":"Many Scotches, especially from Islay, are peated. Most bourbon has none: char from the barrel is different and reads as toasty rather than smoky."}},{"key":"heat","label":"Alcohol heat","scale":["None","L","M","H"],"tip":{"what":"The prickle of ethanol in the nose.","how":"Some heat is expected, more so at high proof. A sharp sting that hides everything else counts against the whiskey."}}]},{"key":"palate","label":"Palate","max":30,"tip":{"what":"What you taste, the biggest section. Includes flavor, weight and how it develops in your mouth.","how":"Take a small first sip to adjust to the alcohol, then a real sip held for a few seconds. Notice the start, the middle and how it changes."},"rows":[{"key":"sweet","label":"Sweet","scale":["None","L","M","H"],"tags":"sweet","tip":{"what":"Sweet flavors: caramel, vanilla, toffee, honey. Much of it comes from the barrel's toasted and charred wood.","how":"Think dessert aisle. Bourbon in new charred oak is usually sweet; many Scotches are less so."}},{"key":"fruit","label":"Fruit","scale":["None","L","M","H"],"tags":"fruit","tip":{"what":"Fruit flavors: fresh fruit (apple, pear, citrus) from fermentation, or dried fruit (raisin, fig) often from sherry casks.","how":"Fresh and bright points to the spirit itself; jammy and dried often points to the cask."}},{"key":"spice","label":"Spice","scale":["None","L","M","H"],"tags":"spice","tip":{"what":"Spice flavors: pepper, cinnamon, clove, mint. Rye grain and oak are the usual sources.","how":"Rye whiskeys lead with spice. A peppery tingle is spice; a burn is alcohol heat."}},{"key":"oak","label":"Oak","scale":["None","L","M","H"],"tags":"oak","tip":{"what":"Wood flavors: toasted oak, char, leather, tobacco, coconut. Stronger with age and with new barrels.","how":"Pleasant oak tastes like a cigar box or a lumber yard. Too much turns bitter and drying."}},{"key":"grain","label":"Grain","scale":["None","L","M","H"],"tags":"grain","tip":{"what":"Grain flavors: corn, bread, cereal, malt. Strongest in young whiskeys and those with less barrel influence.","how":"Breakfast cereal and bread crust. Corn whiskey and young craft whiskeys show it most."}},{"key":"smoke","label":"Smoke","scale":["None","L","M","H"],"tags":"smoke","spotlight":["scotch"],"spotlightNote":"Peat smoke is common in Scotch, especially from Islay.","tip":{"what":"Smoke flavors: usually peat, from malted barley dried over peat fires. Can read as campfire, ash, iodine, seaweed or medicinal.","how":"Many Scotches, especially from Islay, are peated. Most bourbon has none: char from the barrel is different and reads as toasty rather than smoky."}},{"key":"body","label":"Body","scale":["Thin","Medium","Oily"],"tip":{"what":"The whiskey's weight and texture.","how":"Thin feels watery. Oily coats your tongue. Pot still and non-chill-filtered whiskeys often feel richer."}}]},{"key":"finish","label":"Finish","max":20,"tip":{"what":"What stays after you swallow. A long, pleasant finish is one of the marks of a great whiskey.","how":"After swallowing, breathe out slowly through your nose and count how long the flavor lasts."},"rows":[{"key":"length","label":"Length","scale":["Short","Medium","Long"],"tip":{"what":"How long the flavor lasts after swallowing.","how":"Short fades in a few seconds. Long lingers for a minute or more."}},{"key":"heat","label":"Heat","scale":["None","L","M","H"],"tip":{"what":"Warmth from alcohol as it goes down.","how":"A gentle warmth down the chest is the classic 'Kentucky hug.' A harsh burn is a fault."}},{"key":"character","label":"Character","scale":["Dry","","Even","","Sweet"],"ends":true,"tags":"finish","tip":{"what":"How the finish leans: dry and drying, or sweet and lingering.","how":"Tag what stays with you: oak, spice, smoke, sweetness."}}]},{"key":"overall","label":"Overall","max":15,"tip":{"what":"Your overall judgment: how well it all fits together and how much you'd want another pour.","how":"Step back from the parts. Would you buy this bottle? Recommend it?"},"rows":[{"key":"balance","label":"Balance","scale":["Integrated","","","","Disjointed"],"ends":true,"tip":{"what":"Whether sweetness, spice, oak, fruit and alcohol work together.","how":"Disjointed whiskey has one element sticking out, like raw alcohol or heavy oak."}},{"key":"complexity","label":"Complexity","scale":["Layered","","","","Simple"],"ends":true,"tip":{"what":"How many distinct things you can find, and whether they change as you sip.","how":"Simple isn't bad, especially at a low price. Layered whiskeys reveal more with time and water."}},{"key":"intangibles","label":"Intangibles","scale":["Memorable","","","","Forgettable"],"ends":true,"tip":{"what":"Drinkability, character, the part a checklist misses.","how":"Would you remember this pour next week?"}}]}],"flaws":[{"key":"hot","label":"Too hot","tip":"Burning, sharp alcohol that hides the flavor.","cause":"Very young whiskey, or high proof without enough flavor to carry it."},{"key":"solvent","label":"Solvent","tip":"Nail polish remover, paint thinner.","cause":"Heavy fusel alcohols from a rushed or poorly cut distillation."},{"key":"overoaked","label":"Over-oaked","tip":"Bitter, tannic, drying wood, like chewing a pencil.","cause":"Too long in an active barrel, or small barrels used to force age quickly."},{"key":"young","label":"Young / grainy","tip":"Raw grain, wet cardboard, new-make spirit character.","cause":"Not enough time in the barrel to round off the spirit."},{"key":"sulfur","label":"Sulfur","tip":"Struck match, rubber, cooked cabbage.","cause":"From distillation, or from sulfur candles used to clean wine casks before filling."},{"key":"musty","label":"Musty / cork","tip":"Damp cardboard, wet basement, moldy.","cause":"Cork taint (TCA) from a bad cork, or a musty cask."},{"key":"metallic","label":"Metallic","tip":"Tinny, coin-like.","cause":"Equipment or water contamination."},{"key":"soapy","label":"Soapy","tip":"Soap or detergent.","cause":"Fatty acids, or a glass with soap residue. Rinse the glass and try again first."},{"key":"sour","label":"Sour","tip":"Vinegar or sour milk.","cause":"Bacterial problems during fermentation."}],"flawLevels":["L","M","H"],"descriptors":{"sweet":["Caramel","Vanilla","Toffee","Brown sugar","Honey","Maple","Butterscotch","Molasses","Chocolate","Marzipan"],"fruit":["Cherry","Orange peel","Lemon","Apple","Pear","Banana","Raisin","Dried fig","Stone fruit","Tropical","Berry","Sherry"],"spice":["Cinnamon","Clove","Nutmeg","Black pepper","Rye spice","Ginger","Mint","Anise","Baking spice"],"oak":["Toasted oak","Char","Cedar","Sawdust","Leather","Tobacco","Coconut","Dill","Tannic"],"grain":["Corn","Bread","Cereal","Biscuit","Malt","Nutty","Porridge"],"smoke":["Campfire","Peat","Ash","Iodine","Medicinal","Seaweed","Brine","Bacon"],"finish":["Warm","Dry","Sweet","Spicy","Oaky","Smoky","Bitter","Clean","Lingering"]},"requireAll":true},{"type":"textarea","bind":"comments","label":"Comments","placeholder":"What stood out? Would you buy the bottle?"}],"bands":[{"min":90,"label":"Exceptional","color":"#2F7A3D","note":"A standout pour"},{"min":80,"label":"Excellent","color":"#4E8A3A","note":"Excellent, with minor quibbles"},{"min":70,"label":"Very good","color":"#C8862B","note":"Very good, worth buying"},{"min":60,"label":"Good","color":"#B26A1F","note":"Good, but unremarkable"},{"min":50,"label":"Fair","color":"#8E3B1F","note":"Noticeable problems"},{"min":0,"label":"Problematic","color":"#7A2020","note":"Major flaws"}],"list":{"defaultSort":"recent","lines":[["maker","styleFull"],["place","date"]],"badges":[{"bind":"answers.proof","tone":"plain","suffix":" proof"},{"bind":"answers.age_label","tone":"plain"}],"commentsIn":"detail","photoInDetail":true,"detailExtras":[{"bind":"answers.label_tags","label":"On the label"},{"bind":"answers.finish_cask","label":"Finished in"},{"bind":"answers.serving","label":"Served"}]},"about":[{"heading":null,"text":"A shared scoresheet for judging whiskey: American, Scotch, Irish, Japanese, Canadian and beyond. WhiskeyList is in beta, so tell us what's missing or wrong."},{"heading":"Where the style rules come from","text":"Style cards summarize each category's public rules: the US Standards of Identity (27 CFR 5.143), Tennessee Code 57-2-106, the Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, the Irish Whiskey Technical File, Canada's Food and Drug Regulations, and the Japan Spirits & Liqueurs Makers Association's labeling standards. Each card links to its source. Flavor notes describe common tendencies, not rules."},{"heading":null,"text":"WhiskeyList isn't affiliated with any distillery or trade body. Please enjoy responsibly."}]}$cfg$::jsonb, 1, now())
on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.topic_versions (slug, version, config)
select slug, 1, published from public.topics where slug = 'whiskey'
on conflict (slug, version) do nothing;

-- Check: should now list beer, whiskey and wings.
select slug, published_version, published->>'name' as name from public.topics order by slug;
